[38;2;124;124;124mActually, a cleaner approach: Let me try to show $f(n)=n$ for all $n\geq 0$ by [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mstrong induction, and then handle negative values.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor the inductive step, assume $f(k)=k$ for all $0\leq k\leq n$. We want to show[0m[38;2;124;124;124m$f(n+1)=n+1$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $n+1$ can be written as $a^2+b^2$ where $|a|,|b|,|a-b|\leq n$ and $|ab|\leq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mn$, then from $(\star)$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$f(n+1)=f(a^2+b^2)=f(ab)+\frac{f(a-b)^2+f(a)^2+f(b)^2}{2}=ab+\frac{(a-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mb)^2+a^2+b^2}{2}=a^2+b^2=n+1$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe conditions are: $a^2+b^2=n+1$, $|ab|\leq n$, $|a-b|\leq n$, $|a|\leq n$, $|[0m
[38;2;124;124;124mb|\leq n$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIf $n+1=a^2+b^2$ with $a,b\geq 0$, then $a,b\leq\sqrt{n+1}\leq n$ (for $n\geq [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m1$), $|a-b|\leq\sqrt{n+1}\leq n$, and $|ab|\leq (n+1)/2\leq n$ (for $n\geq 1$). [0m[38;2;124;124;124mSo the conditions are satisfied.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mBut not every $n+1$ is a sum of two squares! So we need another approach for [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthose.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFor $n+1$ not a sum of two squares, we can use the three-variable equation. If [0m
